__d(function(e,a,o,n,i,t,r){i.exports={grammar:{title:"Patois grammar",tileSub:"The little words that do the work",intro:["Patois has a grammar of its own, with clear rules that speakers follow every day. It isn't broken English: in many ways it is more regular than English.","Verbs never change. Mi go, yuh go, im go, dem go: no -s, no -ed, no -ing, and no am, is or are.","Small words before the verb do the work instead. A means it is happening now, did puts it in the past, a go and wi point to the future, and nuh, neva and cyaa make it negative.","Learn these few words and you can build almost any sentence. Each pattern has its own page with examples and a quick practice."],listTitle:"Patterns",listNote:"The ones you get wrong come up more often in mixed practice.",rulesTitle:"How it works",done:["Brilliant. The little words are falling into place.","Good work. The ones you missed will come round again.","Everyone mixes these up at first. Read the pattern pages, then have another go."],items:[{id:"a-prog",name:"a (doing now)",local:"a + verb",q:"mi a nyam",job:"Put a before a verb to say something is happening right now, like English 'I'm eating' or 'she's cooking'. The verb itself never changes.",triggers:["right now, at the moment","Wah yuh a do? (What are you doing?)","Mi a come! (I'm coming!)"],example:"Mi *a nyam*. Wah yuh *a do*?",rule:"Person + a + verb. No -ing, and no am, is or are: Mi a cook, Shi a sleep, Dem a play football.",watch:"In the west of Jamaica you may hear deh instead of a: Mi deh nyam. It means the same thing.",samples:[{base:"nyam",form:"a nyam"},{base:"cook",form:"a cook"},{base:"come",form:"a come"}]},{id:"a-go",name:"a go (going to)",local:"a go + verb",q:"mi a go cook",job:"Put a go before a verb to say what is going to happen, especially plans and things about to happen.",triggers:["plans: tumaro, later, next week","Wah yuh a go do? (What are you going to do?)","It a go rain. (It's going to rain.)"],example:"Mi *a go* cook tumaro. Wah yuh *a go* do?",rule:"Person + a go + verb: Mi a go try, Im a go call yuh. To say you are going somewhere, go comes twice: Mi a go go a town.",watch:"You will often see it written as one word, ago or aguh. It has nothing to do with English ago.",samples:[{base:"cook",form:"a go cook"},{base:"call",form:"a go call"},{base:"try",form:"a go try"}]},{id:"wi",name:"wi (will)",local:"wi + verb",q:"mi wi call yuh",job:"Wi before a verb means 'will': a promise, an offer or a prediction.",triggers:["promises and offers: I'll help","predictions: she'll love it","Wi wi see. (We'll see.)"],example:"Mi *wi* call yuh. Wi *wi* see.",rule:"Person + wi + verb: Mi wi help yuh, Shi wi come. Wi is also the word for we, so Wi wi go means We will go.",watch:"A go is for plans and things about to happen; wi is for promises and predictions. Often either one works.",samples:[{base:"help",form:"wi help"},{base:"come",form:"wi come"},{base:"see",form:"wi see"}]},{id:"did",name:"did (past)",local:"did + verb",q:"mi did deh deh",job:"Did before a verb puts it in the past. You need it most with words like know, want, have and deh, which can't show the past on their own.",triggers:["was, were: Mi did deh deh (I was there)","knew, wanted, had","describing the past: Di food did nice"],example:"Mi *did* deh a yaad. Shi *did* waan go.",rule:"Person + did + verb: Mi did know, Dem did waan come. Action words can show the past without it: Mi see im (I saw him).",watch:"This did doesn't make questions, as in English 'Did you go?'. Some speakers, especially in the country, say ben or en instead: Mi ben deh deh.",samples:[{base:"deh",form:"did deh"},{base:"know",form:"did know"},{base:"waan",form:"did waan"}]},{id:"neva",name:"neva (didn't)",local:"neva + verb",q:"mi neva go",job:"Neva before a verb means 'didn't'. It is the usual way to say something did not happen.",triggers:["yessideh, last night, last week","Mi neva know. (I didn't know.)","Wi neva reach yet. (We haven't got there yet.)"],example:"Mi *neva* see im yessideh. Shi *neva* know!",rule:"Person + neva + verb: Im neva come, Yuh neva call mi! The verb stays the same.",watch:"Neva usually just means didn't, about one time, not 'never ever'. Mi neva go means I didn't go.",samples:[{base:"go",form:"neva go"},{base:"come",form:"neva come"},{base:"know",form:"neva know"}]},{id:"nuh",name:"nuh (don't, not)",local:"nuh + verb",q:"mi nuh know",job:"Nuh makes things negative now: don't, doesn't, isn't, aren't. It goes before a verb or a describing word.",triggers:["don't, doesn't","isn't, aren't: Mi nuh hungry","orders: Nuh worry! (Don't worry!)"],example:"Mi *nuh* know. Shi *nuh* like fish.",rule:"Person + nuh + verb: Mi nuh waan it, Im nuh deh yah. The same nuh for everyone: no don't or doesn't.",watch:"Nuh is for now. For the past use neva: Mi nuh go means I don't go, Mi neva go means I didn't go.",samples:[{base:"know",form:"nuh know"},{base:"like",form:"nuh like"},{base:"hungry",form:"nuh hungry"}]},{id:"cyaa",name:"cyaa (can't)",local:"cyaa + verb",q:"mi cyaa swim",job:"Cyaa before a verb means can't: you're not able to, or you're not allowed to.",triggers:["can't, cannot","Mi cyaa badda. (I can't be bothered.)","Yuh cyaa park yah. (You can't park here.)"],example:"Mi *cyaa* swim. Mi *cyaa* hear yuh.",rule:"Person + cyaa + verb: Mi cyaa wait, Im cyaa come tideh. The positive is can: Mi can swim.",watch:"Listen to the vowel: can is short (kyan), cyaa is long and said through the nose (kyaahn).",samples:[{base:"swim",form:"cyaa swim"},{base:"come",form:"cyaa come"},{base:"hear",form:"cyaa hear"}]},{id:"don",name:"don (already)",local:"don + verb",q:"mi don nyam",job:"Don before a verb says something is already finished.",triggers:["already, finished","Mi don tell yuh! (I've already told you!)","Yuh don? (Have you finished?)"],example:"Mi *don* nyam. Di food *don* cook.",rule:"Person + don + verb: Wi don reach, Shi don go home. On its own, don means finished: Mi don (I've finished).",watch:"Don is not don't. Mi don go means I've already gone; Mi nuh go means I don't go.",samples:[{base:"nyam",form:"don nyam"},{base:"cook",form:"don cook"},{base:"reach",form:"don reach"}]},{id:"deh",name:"deh (be somewhere)",local:"deh + place",q:"mi deh yah",job:"Deh means 'be' when you say where someone or something is: at home, here, in London.",triggers:["where someone or something is","Weh yuh deh? (Where are you?)","Mi deh yah. (I'm here, and also: I'm fine.)"],example:"Mi *deh* a yaad. Weh yuh *deh*?",rule:"Person or thing + deh + place: Shi deh a work, Di book deh pon di table. Deh deh means be there: Im deh deh.",watch:"Deh is only for places. For what someone is, use a (Mi a teacha); with describing words, use nothing (Mi tired).",samples:[{base:"yaad",form:"deh a yaad"},{base:"yah",form:"deh yah"},{base:"work",form:"deh a work"}]},{id:"a-is",name:"a (is, before a noun)",local:"a + noun",q:"mi a teacha",job:"Before a noun, a links a person or thing to what it is, like 'am, is, are': I'm a teacher, she's my sister.",triggers:["jobs: Shi a nurse","who someone is: Im a mi bredda","A mi. (It's me.) A who dat? (Who's that?)"],example:"Mi *a* teacha. Dis *a* mi mumma.",rule:"Person or thing + a + noun: Yuh a mi fren, Dat a mi yaad. Questions often start with it: A wah dis? (What's this?)",watch:"This a only goes before nouns. Before a verb, a means doing now (Mi a nyam); before a describing word, you need nothing (Mi tired).",samples:[{base:"teacha",form:"a teacha"},{base:"nurse",form:"a nurse"},{base:"mi bredda",form:"a mi bredda"}]},{id:"zero",name:"Nothing before describing words",local:"\u2205 + describing word",q:"di food nice",job:"To describe someone or something, put the describing word straight after it. There is no word for 'is', 'am' or 'are' here.",triggers:["how someone feels: Mi tired, Mi hungry","what something is like: Di food nice","colours and looks: Di car blue"],example:"Di food *nice*. Mi *hungry*.",rule:"Person or thing + describing word: Shi tall, Di house big, Dem happy. To make it negative, add nuh: Di food nuh hot.",watch:"Don't add a or deh: say Mi tired, not Mi a tired or Mi deh tired.",samples:[{base:"nice",form:"di food nice"},{base:"tired",form:"mi tired"},{base:"tall",form:"shi tall"}]},{id:"fi",name:"fi (mine; to)",local:"fi + person, fi + verb",q:"fi mi",job:"Fi has two everyday jobs. Before mi, yuh, im and the rest it means mine, yours, his. Before a verb it means 'to'.",triggers:["mine, yours, ours: fi mi, fi yuh, fi wi","A fi who dis? (Whose is this?)","to + verb: ready fi go, come fi help"],example:"Dis a *fi mi*. Mi ready *fi* go.",rule:"fi + person = mine, yours: fi mi, fi yuh, fi im, fi wi, fi unu, fi dem. fi + verb = to: Im come fi help.",watch:"Before a noun, plain mi is enough: mi phone (my phone). After waan, fi is optional: Mi waan go and Mi waan fi go both work.",samples:[{base:"mi",form:"fi mi"},{base:"yuh",form:"fi yuh"},{base:"go",form:"ready fi go"}]},{id:"dem",name:"dem (plural)",local:"noun + dem",q:"di pickney dem",job:"Dem after a noun makes it plural, like English -s. The noun itself doesn't change.",triggers:["the people or things you mean: di pickney dem","mi fren dem (my friends)","Weh di key dem deh? (Where are the keys?)"],example:"*Di pickney dem* deh a school. Mi love mi *fren dem*.",rule:"di + noun + dem: di gyal dem, di teacha dem. With a number you can leave dem out: two bredda (two brothers).",watch:"On its own, dem means they or them: Dem deh yah (They're here). After a name it means and the others: Marcia dem (Marcia and her lot).",samples:[{base:"pickney",form:"di pickney dem"},{base:"gyal",form:"di gyal dem"},{base:"fren",form:"mi fren dem"}]}],frames:[{c:"a-prog",text:"Mi ___ nyam.",answer:"a",wrong:["did","don","neva"],m:"I'm eating.",why:"It's happening right now, so a goes before the verb."},{c:"a-prog",text:"Wah yuh ___ do?",answer:"a",wrong:["did","nuh"],m:"What are you doing?",why:"Asking about right now: a before the verb, with no are and no -ing."},{c:"a-prog",text:"Di pickney dem ___ play football.",answer:"a",wrong:["did","neva","don"],m:"The children are playing football.",why:"Dem makes it plural; a says it is happening now."},{c:"a-prog",text:"Mi ___ come!",answer:"a",wrong:["did","neva"],m:"I'm coming!",why:"You're on your way right now: Mi a come."},{c:"a-prog",text:"Wi ___ wait fi di bus.",answer:"a",wrong:["neva","don","did"],m:"We're waiting for the bus.",why:"Still going on as you speak, so it takes a."},{c:"a-go",text:"Mi ___ cook tumaro.",answer:"a go",wrong:["did","don","neva"],m:"I'm going to cook tomorrow.",why:"A plan for later: a go before the verb."},{c:"a-go",text:"Wah yuh ___ do tumaro?",answer:"a go",wrong:["did","neva","don"],m:"What are you going to do tomorrow?",why:"Tumaro is in the future, so it takes a go."},{c:"a-go",text:"Im ___ call yuh.",answer:"a go",wrong:["did","neva","don"],m:"He's going to call you.",why:"Something about to happen: a go, and the verb stays the same."},{c:"a-go",text:"Mi ___ go a town.",answer:"a go",wrong:["did","neva","don"],m:"I'm going to go to town.",why:"The first go belongs to a go, the future; the second go is the verb."},{c:"a-go",text:"Wi ___ miss yuh!",answer:"a go",wrong:["did","neva","don"],m:"We're going to miss you!",why:"It hasn't happened yet: a go points to the future."},{c:"wi",text:"Mi ___ call yuh tumaro.",answer:"wi",wrong:["did","neva","don"],m:"I'll call you tomorrow.",why:"A promise about the future: wi means will."},{c:"wi",text:"Wi ___ see.",answer:"wi",wrong:["did","neva"],m:"We'll see.",why:"The first wi means we, the second means will."},{c:"wi",text:"Nuh worry, im ___ come.",answer:"wi",wrong:["did","neva","don"],m:"Don't worry, he'll come.",why:"A prediction: wi before the verb."},{c:"wi",text:"Mi ___ help yuh.",answer:"wi",wrong:["neva","did","don"],m:"I'll help you.",why:"An offer or a promise takes wi."},{c:"wi",text:"Dem ___ love it.",answer:"wi",wrong:["did","neva","don"],m:"They'll love it.",why:"Saying what will happen: wi, and love stays the same."},{c:"did",text:"Mi ___ deh a yaad.",answer:"did",wrong:["nuh","a go"],m:"I was at home.",why:"Deh means be somewhere; did puts it in the past: was."},{c:"did",text:"Shi ___ waan go.",answer:"did",wrong:["nuh","wi"],m:"She wanted to go.",why:"Waan can't change to show the past, so did does it."},{c:"did",text:"Mi ___ know dat.",answer:"did",wrong:["nuh","wi"],m:"I knew that.",why:"Know never becomes knew: did does that job."},{c:"did",text:"Dem ___ deh deh.",answer:"did",wrong:["nuh","a go"],m:"They were there.",why:"Deh deh means be there; did makes it were."},{c:"did",text:"Wi ___ have a dog.",answer:"did",wrong:["nuh","a go"],m:"We had a dog.",why:"Have stays the same; did tells you it is in the past."},{c:"neva",text:"Mi ___ know!",answer:"neva",wrong:["nuh","did"],m:"I didn't know!",why:"Neva is past: I didn't know. Mi nuh know means I don't know."},{c:"neva",text:"Mi ___ see im yessideh.",answer:"neva",wrong:["did","a go","don"],m:"I didn't see him yesterday.",why:"Yesterday, and it didn't happen: neva before the verb."},{c:"neva",text:"Im ___ come a school.",answer:"neva",wrong:["did","a go","don"],m:"He didn't come to school.",why:"Something that didn't happen takes neva."},{c:"neva",text:"Wi ___ hear yuh.",answer:"neva",wrong:["did","wi"],m:"We didn't hear you.",why:"Neva means didn't, not only never."},{c:"neva",text:"Shi ___ like it.",answer:"neva",wrong:["did","wi"],m:"She didn't like it.",why:"Past and negative together: neva."},{c:"nuh",text:"Mi ___ know.",answer:"nuh",wrong:["neva","did"],m:"I don't know.",why:"Nuh before the verb means don't. For the past you'd say neva."},{c:"nuh",text:"Shi ___ like fish.",answer:"nuh",wrong:["neva","did"],m:"She doesn't like fish.",why:"The same nuh for everyone: no doesn't or don't."},{c:"nuh",text:"Yuh ___ haffi worry.",answer:"nuh",wrong:["neva","did"],m:"You don't have to worry.",why:"Nuh goes before haffi, just as before any verb."},{c:"nuh",text:"Di food ___ nice.",answer:"nuh",wrong:["neva","deh"],m:"The food isn't nice.",why:"With describing words, nuh means isn't."},{c:"nuh",text:"___ do dat!",answer:"nuh",wrong:["did","cyaa"],m:"Don't do that!",why:"At the start, nuh gives an order: don't."},{c:"nuh",text:"Im ___ deh yah.",answer:"nuh",wrong:["neva","a"],m:"He isn't here.",why:"Nuh with deh means isn't here; neva deh would be wasn't."},{c:"cyaa",text:"Mi ___ swim.",answer:"cyaa",wrong:["nuh","neva"],m:"I can't swim.",why:"Can't is cyaa, before the verb."},{c:"cyaa",text:"Mi ___ find mi phone.",answer:"cyaa",wrong:["nuh","don"],m:"I can't find my phone.",why:"You've looked and you can't: cyaa."},{c:"cyaa",text:"Mi ___ hear yuh.",answer:"cyaa",wrong:["nuh","neva"],m:"I can't hear you.",why:"Cyaa means can't; hear stays the same."},{c:"cyaa",text:"Dem ___ come tideh.",answer:"cyaa",wrong:["neva","did"],m:"They can't come today.",why:"Not able to: cyaa."},{c:"cyaa",text:"Yuh ___ park yah.",answer:"cyaa",wrong:["neva","did"],m:"You can't park here.",why:"Cyaa also means you're not allowed."},{c:"don",text:"Mi ___ nyam.",answer:"don",wrong:["a","a go","nuh"],m:"I've already eaten.",why:"Already finished: don before the verb."},{c:"don",text:"Wi ___ reach.",answer:"don",wrong:["a go","neva"],m:"We've already arrived.",why:"Reach means arrive; don says it has already happened."},{c:"don",text:"Shi ___ cook di food.",answer:"don",wrong:["a go","neva"],m:"She's already cooked the food.",why:"It's done and finished, so don."},{c:"don",text:"Mi ___ pay.",answer:"don",wrong:["a go","cyaa"],m:"I've already paid.",why:"Don, not don't: it means already."},{c:"don",text:"Mi ___ tell yuh!",answer:"don",wrong:["a go","cyaa"],m:"I've already told you!",why:"It has already happened: don."},{c:"deh",text:"Mi ___ a yaad.",answer:"deh",wrong:["a","fi"],m:"I'm at home.",why:"Saying where someone is: deh."},{c:"deh",text:"Weh yuh ___?",answer:"deh",wrong:["a","fi"],m:"Where are you?",why:"Where questions end with deh: Weh yuh deh?"},{c:"deh",text:"Di book ___ pon di table.",answer:"deh",wrong:["a","did"],m:"The book's on the table.",why:"Things have a place too: deh."},{c:"deh",text:"Wi ___ inna London.",answer:"deh",wrong:["a","dem"],m:"We're in London.",why:"Deh plus a place: inna London, a yaad, a work."},{c:"deh",text:"Mi ___ yah.",answer:"deh",wrong:["a","fi"],m:"I'm here.",why:"Mi deh yah means I'm here, and it's also a way of saying I'm fine."},{c:"a-is",text:"Mi ___ teacha.",answer:"a",wrong:["deh","fi"],m:"I'm a teacher.",why:"Before a noun, a means am, is or are."},{c:"a-is",text:"Shi ___ mi sista.",answer:"a",wrong:["deh","fi"],m:"She's my sister.",why:"Saying who someone is: a + noun."},{c:"a-is",text:"Dis ___ fi mi.",answer:"a",wrong:["did","dem"],m:"This is mine.",why:"Dis a means this is."},{c:"a-is",text:"___ who dat?",answer:"a",wrong:["deh","nuh"],m:"Who's that?",why:"Who and what questions often start with a: A who dat?"},{c:"a-is",text:"Mi bredda ___ docta.",answer:"a",wrong:["deh","fi"],m:"My brother's a doctor.",why:"Jobs take a: Mi bredda a docta."},{c:"zero",text:"Di food ___.",answer:"nice",wrong:["a nice","deh nice"],m:"The food is nice.",why:"No word for is before a describing word: Di food nice."},{c:"zero",text:"Mi ___.",answer:"tired",wrong:["a tired","deh tired"],m:"I'm tired.",why:"Saying how you feel needs no am: Mi tired."},{c:"zero",text:"Shi ___.",answer:"tall",wrong:["a tall","deh tall"],m:"She's tall.",why:"Describing someone: just the describing word."},{c:"zero",text:"Di sun ___ tideh.",answer:"hot",wrong:["a hot","deh hot"],m:"The sun's hot today.",why:"No is needed: Di sun hot."},{c:"zero",text:"Dem ___.",answer:"happy",wrong:["a happy","deh happy"],m:"They're happy.",why:"No are needed either: Dem happy."},{c:"fi",text:"Dis a ___ mi.",answer:"fi",wrong:["a","dem"],m:"This is mine.",why:"Fi mi means mine."},{c:"fi",text:"A ___ who dis?",answer:"fi",wrong:["a","deh"],m:"Whose is this?",why:"Fi who means whose: A fi who dis?"},{c:"fi",text:"Mi ready ___ go.",answer:"fi",wrong:["deh","dem"],m:"I'm ready to go.",why:"Before a verb, fi means to."},{c:"fi",text:"Mi come ___ see yuh.",answer:"fi",wrong:["did","dem"],m:"I came to see you.",why:"Fi + verb gives the reason: to see you."},{c:"fi",text:"Dat a ___ yuh.",answer:"fi",wrong:["a","deh"],m:"That's yours.",why:"Fi yuh means yours."},{c:"dem",text:"Di pickney ___ deh a school.",answer:"dem",wrong:["a","dis"],m:"The children are at school.",why:"Dem after the noun makes it plural."},{c:"dem",text:"Mi love mi bredda ___.",answer:"dem",wrong:["a","fi"],m:"I love my brothers.",why:"No -s in Patois: add dem after the noun."},{c:"dem",text:"Weh di key ___ deh?",answer:"dem",wrong:["a","dis"],m:"Where are the keys?",why:"More than one key: di key dem."},{c:"dem",text:"Di gyal ___ a sing.",answer:"dem",wrong:["a","fi"],m:"The girls are singing.",why:"Dem shows more than one; a shows it is happening now."},{c:"dem",text:"___ deh yah.",answer:"dem",wrong:["unu","im"],m:"They're here.",why:"On its own, dem means they or them."}]},alphabet:{intro:"Patois shares many words with English but has its own sounds: no th, h usually dropped, long vowels, and a few vowels said through the nose. Each new word in this course comes with a 'How to say it' line in the JLU phonetic spelling. The JLU system, from the Jamaican Language Unit at the University of the West Indies, writes each sound with the same letters every time. Learn these spellings once and you can read any word aloud. Tap a sound to hear it in a word.",letters:[{l:"i",name:"i as in sit",ex:"pikni",m:"child",tip:"Everyday spelling often doubles the letters around it: pickney, likkle (likl)."},{l:"e",name:"e as in bed",ex:"de",m:"there; be somewhere",tip:"Everyday spelling adds a silent h: deh, weh (we), seh (se)."},{l:"a",name:"a as in cat",ex:"dakta",m:"doctor",tip:"Also used where British English has the short o of hot and dog: dakta (docta), pan (pon)."},{l:"o",name:"u as in cut",ex:"kom",m:"come",tip:"JLU o is the vowel of cut and come, never the o of hot. Everyday spelling keeps the English: come, undastan (ondastan)."},{l:"u",name:"u as in put",ex:"yu",m:"you",tip:"Everyday spelling often writes uh or oo: yuh, ooman (uman)."},{l:"ii",name:"ee as in see",ex:"iina",m:"in, into",tip:"A long i. Everyday spelling: inna."},{l:"aa",name:"a as in father",ex:"maanin",m:"morning",tip:"Everyday spelling often uses aw: mawnin. Also yaad (home) and taak (talk)."},{l:"uu",name:"oo as in food",ex:"skuul",m:"school",tip:"Everyday spelling usually keeps the English: school, food (fuud)."},{l:"ie",name:"ee-eh, in one go",ex:"rien",m:"rain",tip:"The Jamaican vowel in rain, cake and day: rien, kiek, die. Everyday spelling keeps the English."},{l:"uo",name:"oo-oh, in one go",ex:"uom",m:"home",tip:"The Jamaican vowel in home, know and road: uom, nuo, ruod."},{l:"ai",name:"i as in time",ex:"taim",m:"time",tip:"Also bwai (bwoy) and rait (write)."},{l:"ou",name:"ow as in now",ex:"nou",m:"now",tip:"Also ou (how) and kou (cow)."},{l:"-hn",name:"through the nose",ex:"kyaahn",m:"can't",tip:"hn is not said as letters: it shows that the vowel before it goes through the nose. Everyday spelling: cyaa or cyaan."},{l:"ch",name:"ch as in chin",ex:"chikin",m:"chicken",tip:"As in English, but always written ch, never tch."},{l:"zh",name:"s as in measure",ex:"mezha",m:"measure",tip:"A rare sound. JLU gives it its own spelling so it is never mixed up with s or z."},{l:"ny",name:"ny as in canyon",ex:"nyam",m:"eat",tip:"Everyday spelling is the same: nyam. Also nyuu (new)."},{l:"ky",name:"c as in cute",ex:"kyaar",m:"car",tip:"Before a, many Jamaicans add a y sound after k. Everyday spelling uses cy: cyar, cyaa."},{l:"gy",name:"g as in argue",ex:"gyal",m:"girl",tip:"Everyday spelling is the same: gyal. Also gyaadn (garden)."},{l:"h",name:"h, only if you hear it",ex:"ous",m:"house",tip:"Most Jamaicans drop h, so JLU writes house as ous and home as uom. In the west, where h is often said, it is written."},{l:"ng",name:"ng as in sing",ex:"lang",m:"long",tip:"At the end of a word, English -ing becomes -in: maanin (mawnin). The ng stays in words like lang and sing."}],combos:[{s:"th \u2192 t",say:"tank yu",ex:"tank yuh",m:"thank you",tip:"Patois has no th sound. The th in thank, thing and think becomes t: tank yuh, ting, tink."},{s:"th \u2192 d",say:"dem",ex:"dem",m:"they, them",tip:"The th in the, this and that becomes d: di, dis, dat, dem."},{s:"no h",say:"uom",ex:"home",m:"home",tip:"Most Jamaicans don't say an h at the start of a word, so home sounds like uom and house like ous. Everyday spelling usually keeps the h."},{s:"-ing \u2192 -in",say:"maanin",ex:"mawnin",m:"morning",tip:"The -ing ending loses its g: mawnin, evenin. Doing now is shown with a instead: Mi a cook."},{s:"-er \u2192 -a",say:"breda",ex:"bredda",m:"brother",tip:"An -er or -or ending is said a: bredda, neva (never), teacha (teacher), docta (doctor)."},{s:"c \u2192 cy",say:"kyaar",ex:"cyar",m:"car",tip:"Before an a sound, k and g often take a y after them: cyar (car), cyaa (can't), gyal (girl)."},{s:"tl \u2192 kl",say:"likl",ex:"likkle",m:"little",tip:"A t before a final l sound becomes k: likkle. Bottle sounds like bakl."},{s:"st \u2192 s",say:"bes",ex:"bes",m:"best",tip:"Two consonants at the end of a word are often cut to one: bes (best), fren (friend), han (hand)."},{s:"dr \u2192 jr",say:"jrink",ex:"drink",m:"drink",tip:"At the start of a word, dr often sounds like jr: drink sounds like jrink and drive like jraiv."},{s:"eh",say:"de",ex:"deh",m:"there; be somewhere",tip:"The h in deh, weh and seh is silent. It only shows a short e, so JLU writes de, we and se."},{s:"uh",say:"yu",ex:"yuh",m:"you",tip:"The h in yuh and nuh is silent too. JLU writes yu and no."},{s:"u \u2192 o",say:"ondastan",ex:"undastan",m:"understand",tip:"The vowel of cut and sun is said further back, close to the o in British hot. JLU writes it o: ondastan, nof (nuff), son (sun)."}]},spelling:{question:"Which is the phonetic (JLU) spelling?",rules:{vowels:{title:"Short vowels",tip:"JLU writes each short vowel with one letter, i e a o u, and drops the extra letters of everyday spelling: pikni (pickney), yu (yuh), de (deh). The o is the vowel of cut: ondastan (undastan)."},long:{title:"Long vowels",tip:"A long vowel is written twice: ii, aa, uu. So inna is iina, mawnin is maanin and school is skuul."},diphthongs:{title:"Gliding vowels",tip:"Four sounds glide from one vowel into another: ie as in rien (rain), uo as in nuo (know), ai as in taim (time) and ou as in nou (now)."},nasal:{title:"Through the nose",tip:"hn after a vowel means it is said through the nose. That's why the everyday cyaa (can't) is kyaahn in JLU."},h:{title:"Dropped h",tip:"JLU writes h only where it is said, and most Jamaicans don't say it: home is uom, house is ous, haffi is afi."},ky:{title:"ky, gy and ny",tip:"JLU writes the y sound you hear after k, g and n: kyaar (car), gyaadn (garden), nyuu (new). Everyday spelling uses cy, or keeps the English."}},items:[{right:"pikni",wrong:"pickney",m:"child",rule:"vowels"},{right:"likl",wrong:"likkle",m:"little",rule:"vowels"},{right:"yu",wrong:"yuh",m:"you",rule:"vowels"},{right:"de",wrong:"deh",m:"there; be somewhere",rule:"vowels"},{right:"we",wrong:"weh",m:"where",rule:"vowels"},{right:"no",wrong:"nuh",m:"not, don't",rule:"vowels"},{right:"Wa gwaan?",wrong:"Wah gwaan?",m:"Hi! (lit. What's going on?)",rule:"vowels"},{right:"pan",wrong:"pon",m:"on",rule:"vowels"},{right:"breda",wrong:"bredda",m:"brother",rule:"vowels"},{right:"ondastan",wrong:"undastan",m:"understand",rule:"vowels"},{right:"nof",wrong:"nuff",m:"plenty, a lot",rule:"vowels"},{right:"dakta",wrong:"docta",m:"doctor",rule:"vowels"},{right:"maanin",wrong:"mawnin",m:"morning",rule:"long"},{right:"iina",wrong:"inna",m:"in, into",rule:"long"},{right:"skuul",wrong:"school",m:"school",rule:"long"},{right:"swiit",wrong:"sweet",m:"sweet",rule:"long"},{right:"kaal",wrong:"call",m:"call",rule:"long"},{right:"fuud",wrong:"food",m:"food",rule:"long"},{right:"taal",wrong:"tall",m:"tall",rule:"long"},{right:"kiek",wrong:"cake",m:"cake",rule:"diphthongs"},{right:"bwai",wrong:"bwoy",m:"boy",rule:"diphthongs"},{right:"taim",wrong:"time",m:"time",rule:"diphthongs"},{right:"nait",wrong:"nite",m:"night",rule:"diphthongs"},{right:"nuo",wrong:"know",m:"know",rule:"diphthongs"},{right:"rien",wrong:"rain",m:"rain",rule:"diphthongs"},{right:"Jamiekan",wrong:"Jamaican",m:"Jamaican (the language)",rule:"diphthongs"},{right:"rait",wrong:"write",m:"write",rule:"diphthongs"},{right:"kyaahn",wrong:"cyaa",m:"can't",rule:"nasal"},{right:"Mi kyaahn swim.",wrong:"Mi cyaa swim.",m:"I can't swim.",rule:"nasal"},{right:"kyaahn bada",wrong:"cyaa badda",m:"can't be bothered",rule:"nasal"},{right:"uom",wrong:"home",m:"home",rule:"h"},{right:"ous",wrong:"house",m:"house",rule:"h"},{right:"afi",wrong:"haffi",m:"have to",rule:"h"},{right:"ongri",wrong:"hungry",m:"hungry",rule:"h"},{right:"ou",wrong:"how",m:"how",rule:"h"},{right:"ier",wrong:"hear",m:"hear",rule:"h"},{right:"kyaar",wrong:"cyar",m:"car",rule:"ky"},{right:"kyan",wrong:"can",m:"can",rule:"ky"},{right:"kyarat",wrong:"carrot",m:"carrot",rule:"ky"},{right:"Skyuuz mi",wrong:"Excuse mi",m:"Excuse me",rule:"ky"}]},variants:{title:"Patois and English",sub:"Same thing, different words",intro:"Patois and British English side by side: the same thing, said two ways. Some Patois words are completely different, like pickney and yaad. Others are English words written the way Jamaicans say them, like tink and bredda. Tap a word to hear it.",places:[{id:"JM",flag:"\ud83c\uddef\ud83c\uddf2",name:"Patois",lang:"en-JM"},{id:"GB",flag:"\ud83c\uddec\ud83c\udde7",name:"British English",lang:"en-GB"}],entries:[{m:"child, children",f:["pickney","child"],kind:"word"},{m:"daughter",f:["gyal pickney","daughter"],kind:"word"},{m:"son",f:["bwoy pickney","son"],kind:"word"},{m:"mum",f:["mumma","mum"],kind:"word"},{m:"dad",f:["puppa","dad"],kind:"word"},{m:"friends",f:["bredren","mates"],kind:"word"},{m:"a group of male friends",f:["mandem","the lads"],kind:"word"},{m:"you (more than one)",f:["unu","you lot"],kind:"word"},{m:"brother",f:["bredda","brother"],kind:"spelling"},{m:"boy",f:["bwoy","boy"],kind:"spelling"},{m:"eat",f:["nyam","eat"],kind:"word"},{m:"takeaway meal",f:["box food","takeaway"],kind:"word"},{m:"a little extra, for free",f:["brawta","a bit extra"],kind:"word"},{m:"market trader",f:["higgler","market trader"],kind:"word"},{m:"avocado",f:["pear","avocado"],kind:"word"},{m:"home",f:["yaad","home"],kind:"word"},{m:"metal roof",f:["zinc roof","corrugated iron roof"],kind:"word"},{m:"electricity bill",f:["light bill","electricity bill"],kind:"word"},{m:"stomach",f:["belly","tummy"],kind:"word"},{m:"leg (and foot)",f:["foot","leg"],kind:"word"},{m:"dress",f:["frock","dress"],kind:"word"},{m:"wind",f:["breeze","wind"],kind:"word"},{m:"angry",f:["vex","angry"],kind:"word"},{m:"scared",f:["frighten","scared"],kind:"word"},{m:"rude, cheeky",f:["facety","cheeky"],kind:"word"},{m:"greedy",f:["craven","greedy"],kind:"word"},{m:"nosy",f:["fass","nosy"],kind:"word"},{m:"posh, stylish",f:["stush","posh"],kind:"word"},{m:"lots, plenty",f:["nuff","loads"],kind:"word"},{m:"arrive",f:["reach","arrive"],kind:"word"},{m:"talk",f:["chat","talk"],kind:"word"},{m:"gossip",f:["labrish","gossip"],kind:"word"},{m:"think",f:["tink","think"],kind:"spelling"},{m:"sit down",f:["siddung","sit down"],kind:"spelling"},{m:"understand",f:["undastan","understand"],kind:"spelling"},{m:"today",f:["tideh","today"],kind:"spelling"},{m:"yesterday",f:["yessideh","yesterday"],kind:"spelling"},{m:"Hi! (lit. What's going on?)",f:["Wah gwaan?","How's it going?"],kind:"word"},{m:"I'm fine (lit. I'm here)",f:["Mi deh yah","I'm fine"],kind:"word"},{m:"See you later",f:["Likkle more","See you later"],kind:"word"},{m:"Take care (lit. walk good)",f:["Walk good","Take care"],kind:"word"},{m:"why",f:["wah mek","why"],kind:"word"},{m:"Let's go!",f:["Mek wi go!","Let's go!"],kind:"word"},{m:"Thank you",f:["Tank yuh","Thank you"],kind:"spelling"},{m:"I can't be bothered.",f:["Mi cyaa badda.","I can't be bothered."],kind:"word"}]},cando:[{id:"greet",level:"A1",emo:"\ud83d\udc4b",text:"Greet people and ask how they are",units:["j1u02"]},{id:"intro",level:"A1",emo:"\ud83d\ude4b",text:"Say who you are and describe people",units:["j1u03","j1u04","j1u05"]},{id:"family",level:"A1",emo:"\ud83d\udc6a",text:"Talk about your family",units:["j1u08","j1u09"]},{id:"food",level:"A1",emo:"\ud83c\udf5b",text:"Talk about food and ask what things cost",units:["j1u07","j1u11"]},{id:"questions",level:"A1",emo:"\u2753",text:"Ask where things are, who and why",units:["j1u06","j1u17"]},{id:"day",level:"A1",emo:"\u23f0",text:"Say what you're doing now and every day",units:["j1u12","j1u14","j1u20"]},{id:"needs",level:"A1",emo:"\ud83d\ude4f",text:"Say what you want, can and have to do",units:["j1u18","j1u19"]},{id:"past",level:"A2",emo:"\ud83d\udcc5",text:"Say what you did and didn't do",units:["j2u01","j2u02","j2u03"]},{id:"plans",level:"A2",emo:"\ud83d\uddd3\ufe0f",text:"Make plans and talk about the future",units:["j2u04","j2u05"]},{id:"market",level:"A2",emo:"\ud83d\uded2",text:"Shop at the market and order at a cookshop",units:["j2u08","j2u09"]},{id:"doctor",level:"A2",emo:"\ud83e\ude7a",text:"Tell a doctor what's wrong",units:["j2u11"]},{id:"feelings",level:"A2",emo:"\u2764\ufe0f",text:"Say how you feel",units:["j2u14"]},{id:"story",level:"B1",emo:"\ud83d\udcd6",text:"Tell a story, Anansi style",units:["j3u01","j3u02","j3u15"]},{id:"reported",level:"B1",emo:"\ud83d\udde3\ufe0f",text:"Pass on what someone said",units:["j3u06"]},{id:"opinions",level:"B1",emo:"\ud83d\udcac",text:"Give your opinion and argue your point",units:["j3u09"]},{id:"switch",level:"B1",emo:"\ud83d\udd00",text:"Know when to use Patois and when to use English",units:["j3u12","j3u13","j3u14"]},{id:"regrets",level:"B2",emo:"\ud83d\udcad",text:"Talk about regrets and what might have been",units:["j4u01"]},{id:"health",level:"B2",emo:"\ud83e\ude7a",text:"Describe how you feel in detail",units:["j4u05"]},{id:"money",level:"B2",emo:"\ud83d\udcb0",text:"Talk money, pardner and making a living",units:["j4u06"]},{id:"rites",level:"B2",emo:"\ud83d\udd6f\ufe0f",text:"Join family occasions with respect",units:["j4u07"]},{id:"cook",level:"B2",emo:"\ud83c\udf72",text:"Follow a recipe the Jamaican way",units:["j4u08"]},{id:"disagree",level:"B2",emo:"\u2696\ufe0f",text:"Disagree with respect",units:["j4u09"]},{id:"read",level:"B2",emo:"\ud83d\udcd6",text:"Read written Patois, everyday and JLU spelling",units:["j4u11"]},{id:"work",level:"B2",emo:"\ud83d\udece\ufe0f",text:"Handle customers and the boss",units:["j4u12"]},{id:"tales",level:"B2",emo:"\ud83d\udd77\ufe0f",text:"Tell an Anansi or duppy story",units:["j4u15"]}],places:[{name:"Kingston",emo:"\ud83c\udfb6",about:"Jamaica's capital and largest city. Government offices moved here from Spanish Town in 1872. Since 2015 Kingston has been a UNESCO Creative City of Music, honouring the city that gave the world ska, rocksteady, reggae and dancehall."},{name:"Port Royal",emo:"\u2693",about:"Once a busy harbour town at the mouth of Kingston Harbour, Port Royal was largely destroyed by an earthquake and tsunami on 7 June 1692, and much of the old town now lies under the sea. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in July 2025."},{name:"Blue Mountains",emo:"\u26f0\ufe0f",about:"Blue Mountain Peak, at 2,256 metres (7,402 ft), is Jamaica's highest point. The Blue and John Crow Mountains became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2015, for their Maroon heritage and for plants found nowhere else. The slopes also grow the famous Blue Mountain coffee."},{name:"Moore Town",emo:"\ud83d\udcef",about:"A Maroon town in the hills of Portland, home to descendants of Africans who escaped slavery and won their freedom. UNESCO recognises its heritage, including Kromanti ceremonies and the abeng, a cow-horn trumpet once used to send messages across the hills."},{name:"Port Antonio",emo:"\ud83d\udef6",about:"A harbour town in Portland whose banana trade in the 1880s also started its tourism. Nearby, the Blue Lagoon gets its deep colour from a depth of about 60 metres. Bamboo rafts that once carried bananas down the Rio Grande now carry visitors, a pastime made popular by the actor Errol Flynn."},{name:"Dunn's River Falls",emo:"\ud83d\udca7",about:"Near Ocho Rios, about 55 metres (180 ft) of terraced falls run straight down into the sea, and visitors climb them from the beach. Ocho Rios means 'eight rivers', but the name probably comes from the Spanish Las Chorreras, 'the waterfalls'."},{name:"Nine Mile",emo:"\ud83c\udfb8",about:"A village in the hills of St Ann where Bob Marley was born on 6 February 1945. He is buried here in a mausoleum, and visitors come from all over the world to pay their respects."},{name:"Seville",emo:"\ud83c\udfdb\ufe0f",about:"Seville Heritage Park, near St Ann's Bay, holds layers of history: a Ta\xedno village, Sevilla la Nueva, a 16th-century Spanish settlement, and a British plantation worked by enslaved Africans. It has been on UNESCO's Tentative List since 2009."},{name:"Montego Bay",emo:"\ud83c\udf34",about:"Known as the Second City, and the place where most visitors arrive. Sam Sharpe, a National Hero, led the 1831 to 1832 Christmas Rebellion against slavery here, and the main square is named after him. Hurricane Melissa flooded parts of the city in October 2025."},{name:"Cockpit Country",emo:"\ud83c\udf3f",about:"Steep limestone hills and deep hollows that sheltered Maroon communities, covered by the island's largest surviving stretch of rainforest. At Accompong, Maroons celebrate Cudjoe's birthday and the 1739 peace treaty every 6 January."},{name:"Treasure Beach",emo:"\ud83c\udfa3",about:"A string of fishing villages on the south coast of St Elizabeth, known as the home of community tourism, with no all-inclusive resorts. St Elizabeth was one of the parishes worst hit by Hurricane Melissa in October 2025."},{name:"Negril",emo:"\ud83c\udf05",about:"At the western tip of the island, Negril spans Westmoreland and Hanover and is known for its long beach and its cliffs at sunset. Hurricane Melissa made landfall in Westmoreland in October 2025, and the area is still recovering."},{name:"Spanish Town",emo:"\ud83c\udf09",about:"Founded by the Spanish in the 1500s, Spanish Town in St Catherine stayed Jamaica's capital under British rule until the government moved to Kingston in 1872. Its old square is ringed by buildings from different centuries, and the Old Iron Bridge, shipped over from England, has crossed the Rio Cobre for more than 200 years."},{name:"Falmouth",emo:"\u2728",about:"The capital of Trelawny, on the north coast, Falmouth was founded in 1769 as a sugar port and is known as one of the Caribbean's best-preserved Georgian towns. Nearby, the Luminous Lagoon glows blue-green at night. Hurricane Melissa hit the town very hard in October 2025, and it is still rebuilding."},{name:"Morant Bay",emo:"\u2696\ufe0f",about:"The capital of St Thomas, in Jamaica's far east. In October 1865 Paul Bogle, a Baptist deacon, led a march on the courthouse here, and the Morant Bay Rebellion was put down with great brutality. Bogle and George William Gordon were hanged, and both were named National Heroes in 1969. St Thomas is also the heartland of Kumina."}]}},1350,[]);